          <p>
            Three things jump out when the powders sit side by side. First, protein per serving is a poor way to compare on its own, because serving sizes range from about 25 g of powder to more than 45 g. A plant blend that delivers 21 g of protein from a 46 g serving is a very different product from an isolate that delivers 25 g from 31 g, even though the headline protein numbers are close. That is why the next section converts everything to protein percentage and cost per 25 g of protein.
          </p>

          <p>
            Second, the certification column has more gaps than most shoppers expect. Many popular powders are perfectly good products that simply do not carry an NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport listing, or carry it only on some flavors. For most recreational lifters that is not a reason to avoid a product, but if you are subject to drug testing it is the deciding column. Third, the sweetener column matters more to some people than any protein number: if sucralose or sugar alcohols upset your stomach, or you simply dislike the taste, that narrows the field quickly. If you want no sweetener at all, our <a href="/best-unflavored-protein-powder/">unflavored protein powder</a> guide covers that corner of the market.
          </p>

          <p>
            What the table cannot tell you is taste and mixability, which are the most common reasons people abandon a tub. We do not run taste panels. Buyer consensus, reflected across years of retailer reviews, is that Optimum Nutrition and Dymatize have broad, well-liked flavor lineups, that unflavored concentrates taste milky and are best hidden in smoothies or oats, and that plant blends have a thicker, earthier texture that some people never get used to. Treat any taste claim, including that summary, as a starting point rather than a guarantee.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-to-compare" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How to Compare Protein Powders: The Five Numbers That Matter
          </h2>

          <p>
            When you compare protein powders, start with the type of protein, because it determines most of what follows. Whey concentrate, whey isolate, casein and plant blends differ in how much protein the base ingredient carries, how quickly it digests, how much lactose it contains and how much leucine you get per gram. Once you know the type, you can compare products within that type on much more even terms. Comparing a casein to a pea blend on price alone, for example, tells you very little.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second number is protein as a percentage of the serving. Divide grams of protein per serving by the serving size in grams. Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard Whey, at 24 g of protein in a scoop of about 31 g, works out to roughly 77 percent protein. Isolates typically land somewhere around 80 to 90 percent, while plant blends with added fiber, gums and flavor systems can fall to 50 percent or lower; Orgain's 21 g in about 46 g is closer to 45 percent. The rest of the scoop is carbohydrate, fat, flavoring, sweetener, thickeners and sometimes added vitamins or greens. None of that is necessarily bad, but you are paying for it per tub.
          </p>

          <p>
            The third number, and the one that settles most comparisons, is cost per 25 g of protein. It folds serving size, servings per tub, protein per serving and price into one figure, and it is the only fair way to compare a big budget tub against a small premium one. The formula and a worked example are in the next section. The fourth number is leucine, the amino acid most closely tied to switching on muscle protein synthesis. Research summarized in the International Society of Sports Nutrition position stand on protein points to roughly 2 to 3 g of leucine per meal as the useful threshold, which a 25 g serving of whey usually reaches and some plant blends reach only at larger servings.
          </p>

          <p>
            The fifth number is not really a number: it is whether the exact product you are buying holds a third-party certification. A certification such as NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport means an independent organization tests the product for substances banned in sport, checks it against its label and audits how it is made. Without one, you are relying on the brand's own quality control. That is often fine, but it is worth knowing which situation you are in. Our article on <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">whether protein powder is safe</a> goes deeper on contaminants and heavy metals, which is the main reason this column matters.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Comparison Benchmarks</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">25 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">A useful standard serving of protein to compare on</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">2-3 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Leucine per meal linked to maximal muscle protein synthesis</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">90%+</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Protein content of a true whey isolate ingredient</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">1.6 g/kg</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Daily intake where muscle gains plateau in Morton et al.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Notice what is not on the list: front-of-tub claims such as clinically proven, anabolic, ultra-premium or pharmaceutical grade. None of these terms has a regulated meaning for protein powder in the US, and none of them changes how much protein you get or how well it is made. Compare protein powders on the five numbers above and you will rarely be misled by marketing language.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Cost per 25 g of Protein: The Formula and a Worked Example
          </h2>

          <p>
            Price per tub is the number retailers put in front of you, and it is close to useless for comparison. Tubs range from about one pound to ten pounds, serving sizes differ by almost half, and protein per serving ranges from about 20 g to 30 g. Cost per 25 g of protein strips all of that out. It answers one question: how much do you pay for a standard serving of actual protein from this product? Once you calculate it for two or three candidates, the cheap-looking tub is often not the cheap one.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE FORMULA</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Cost per 25 g = tub price divided by total grams of protein in the tub, multiplied by 25.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Total grams of protein in the tub is protein per serving multiplied by servings per container, both of which are on the label. If a tub lists a range of servings, use the lower number to stay conservative.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Here is a worked example with two hypothetical powders, using made-up round numbers rather than any real product's price. Powder A costs $60 and contains 74 servings of 24 g of protein each, so the tub holds 1,776 g of protein. Divide $60 by 1,776 g and multiply by 25, and Powder A costs about 84 cents per 25 g of protein. Powder B costs $50, which looks cheaper, but contains 30 servings of 21 g each, or 630 g of protein in total. Divide $50 by 630 g and multiply by 25, and Powder B costs about $1.98 per 25 g of protein, more than twice as much.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Hypothetical powder</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Tub price</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Servings</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Protein per serving</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Total protein in tub</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Cost per 25 g</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Powder A (large whey tub)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$60</td>
