          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            A useful protein powder comparison starts with protein type and ends with cost per 25 g of protein. In between, check protein as a share of the scoop, leucine per serving, the order of the ingredient list and whether the exact product carries a certification you have verified yourself. Those few checks separate well-made powders from expensive marketing far more reliably than any front-of-tub claim.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            For most people who digest dairy well, a reputable whey blend or isolate is the best value. For dairy-free diets, a tested plant blend is the answer. Drug-tested athletes should start from the NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport database and compare only what is listed. Whatever you choose, it only works if you hit your daily protein target and train consistently, so buy the powder you will actually keep drinking.
